WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.494
The new path through the park opens on Saturday.

2
00:00:05.494 --> 00:00:11.140
On Friday evening, the keeper walks it once more, from the gate to the station, with a clipboard.

3
00:00:11.367 --> 00:00:13.356
Does every lamp come on at dusk?

4
00:00:13.356 --> 00:00:16.215
Does the little bridge hold a heavy bin lorry?

5
00:00:16.215 --> 00:00:19.323
Does the drain by the pond take a bucket of water?

6
00:00:19.533 --> 00:00:22.273
Each part passed its own test when it was built.

7
00:00:22.273 --> 00:00:25.584
The walk is the first time they all have to work together.

8
00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:27.610
Skipping the walk is tempting.

9
00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:31.050
Every piece was checked, so the whole thing must be fine.

10
00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:35.697
That's exactly how a lamp ends up wired to the same switch as the sprinklers.

11
00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:38.163
Your capstone is that walk.

12
00:00:38.163 --> 00:00:43.767
One small code change travels from your laptop to users, through every part of this course.

13
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:45.952
Fourteen stages, in order.

14
00:00:45.952 --> 00:00:52.333
Foundations, containers, CI, and so on, all the way to security, AI and the platform.

15
00:00:52.567 --> 00:00:54.896
Stage twelve, Dynatrace, is optional.

16
00:00:54.896 --> 00:00:59.492
Do it last, so its trial clock starts when everything else already works.

17
00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:00.529
Three rules.

18
00:01:00.529 --> 00:01:01.358
Go in order.

19
00:01:01.358 --> 00:01:05.643
A stage is done when its check passes, not when it looks fine.

20
00:01:05.643 --> 00:01:07.577
And commit after each stage.

21
00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:10.347
But what counts as a check?

22
00:01:10.347 --> 00:01:11.965
Take stage seven, GitOps.

23
00:01:11.965 --> 00:01:14.359
Here are two ways to write its check.

24
00:01:14.567 --> 00:01:17.197
Pause and guess: which one could ever fail?

25
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.425
Argo CD works can't clearly fail.

26
00:01:21.425 --> 00:01:23.817
You'd just shrug and say it looks fine.

27
00:01:23.817 --> 00:01:25.780
So passing it tells you nothing.

28
00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:29.645
The strong one looks at the running system, after a real merge.

29
00:01:29.867 --> 00:01:31.139
So here's the rule.

30
00:01:31.139 --> 00:01:33.818
A check that cannot fail proves nothing.

31
00:01:33.818 --> 00:01:36.095
Make every check able to say FAIL.

32
00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:40.752
A good check is observable: it looks at the running system.

33
00:01:40.752 --> 00:01:42.159
Binary: pass or fail.

34
00:01:42.159 --> 00:01:44.905
And runnable: one command you can repeat.

35
00:01:45.667 --> 00:01:49.549
Let's turn one line of the checklist into such a check.

36
00:01:49.549 --> 00:01:54.490
Stage two: the image runs as a non-root user, and reports its version.

37
00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:58.442
Ask the image itself, not your memory of the Dockerfile.

38
00:01:58.442 --> 00:01:59.219
User 10001.

39
00:01:59.433 --> 00:02:00.710
Then make it binary.

40
00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:02.943
This little if prints PASS or FAIL.

41
00:02:02.943 --> 00:02:07.475
Every line of the lab's capstone check script follows the same pattern.

42
00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:11.960
The script turns eleven of the fourteen stages into checks.

43
00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:14.956
Run it this early, and almost everything fails.

44
00:02:14.956 --> 00:02:17.378
One passed, eight failed, two skipped.

45
00:02:17.378 --> 00:02:18.270
That's normal.

46
00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:20.628
Read it as your to-do list, in order.

47
00:02:20.833 --> 00:02:23.350
Skip means a tool or address is missing.

48
00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:27.439
And the script exits with one on any failure, so CI could run it.

49
00:02:27.667 --> 00:02:28.483
Quick one.

50
00:02:28.483 --> 00:02:31.422
Argo CD fails, and Prometheus fails.

51
00:02:31.422 --> 00:02:33.299
Which do you fix first?

52
00:02:33.299 --> 00:02:34.605
Pause and guess.

53
00:02:36.833 --> 00:02:37.409
Argo CD.

54
00:02:37.409 --> 00:02:39.280
The deploy feeds the rest.

55
00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:43.598
If the app isn't deployed, Prometheus has nothing to scrape.

56
00:02:43.598 --> 00:02:46.332
Stages depend on the ones before them.

57
00:02:47.833 --> 00:02:49.450
Now a tempting shortcut.

58
00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:51.740
Say stages one to eight are green.

59
00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:53.896
You've just finished stage nine.

60
00:02:53.896 --> 00:02:57.533
Why not run only the check for the stage you just did?

61
00:02:57.533 --> 00:02:58.341
It's faster.

62
00:02:58.567 --> 00:03:00.680
Let's be fair to it, and try.

63
00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:06.363
Say the port-forward you started for stage nine's dashboard grabbed port 8081.

64
00:03:06.363 --> 00:03:08.549
Stage nine's own check passes.

65
00:03:08.767 --> 00:03:11.568
Pause and guess: is everything still green?

66
00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:14.047
No.

67
00:03:14.047 --> 00:03:19.159
The old stage six check, the app on localhost 8081, now fails.

68
00:03:19.159 --> 00:03:22.375
The shortcut would never have told you.

69
00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:28.454
Later stages often break earlier ones: a new policy rejects Redis, a port gets taken.

70
00:03:28.454 --> 00:03:31.484
So rerun the whole script after every stage.

71
00:03:31.484 --> 00:03:34.652
The keeper tries every lamp, not just today's.

72
00:03:36.267 --> 00:03:39.052
Checks prove the path works when things go right.

73
00:03:39.052 --> 00:03:42.065
A game day finds out what happens when they go wrong.

74
00:03:42.065 --> 00:03:47.295
You break something on purpose, like pouring a bucket into the drain before the first storm.

75
00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:50.453
If you do one drill, scale Redis to zero.

76
00:03:50.453 --> 00:04:00.463
But first, write down what you expect: 503 from the readiness check, unready Pods, an alert, error logs in Kibana, a failed span in Jaeger.

77
00:04:00.667 --> 00:04:03.212
Afterwards, write down what really happened.

78
00:04:03.212 --> 00:04:06.798
Wherever the two differ, your picture of the system was wrong.

79
00:04:06.798 --> 00:04:10.905
That's the part worth writing up, and the story interviewers like most.

80
00:04:11.733 --> 00:04:12.785
One honest limit.

81
00:04:12.785 --> 00:04:16.682
The capstone runs on kind, the practice cluster on your laptop.

82
00:04:16.682 --> 00:04:21.322
Real traffic, a real cloud bill and a night on call are still ahead of you.

83
00:04:21.322 --> 00:04:23.920
That's what the next three months are for.

84
00:04:24.633 --> 00:04:28.784
The moral of the story: walk the whole path before opening night.

85
00:04:28.784 --> 00:04:30.572
Done means the check passes.

86
00:04:30.572 --> 00:04:33.126
Checks are observable, binary, runnable.

87
00:04:33.126 --> 00:04:35.233
Rerun them all after every stage.

88
00:04:35.233 --> 00:04:38.043
And break it on purpose, expectations first.

89
00:04:38.867 --> 00:04:39.858
One last check.

90
00:04:39.858 --> 00:04:43.822
You want to show an interviewer that you can do DevOps work.

91
00:04:43.822 --> 00:04:46.135
Which gives the strongest evidence?

92
00:04:46.135 --> 00:04:47.192
Pause and think.

93
00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:52.847
A repository where a commit becomes a checked deploy.

94
00:04:52.847 --> 00:04:56.490
Run the capstone check now, even if most lines say FAIL.

95
00:04:56.490 --> 00:04:58.051
That list is your route.

96
00:04:58.051 --> 00:05:00.392
Thanks for walking the path with me.
